  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nLow potential for showers and a rumble of thunder this \nafternoon/evening\n\nThis afternoon into the evening, area of 850mb WAA currently over \ncentral IA will sneak into far southwestern WI and adjacent areas of \nnortheast IA. With steep lapse rates in the 750-500mb layer per the \n13.18z DVN RAOb, a few showers and perhaps a thunderstorm may \ndevelop. That said, continued capping concerns even when lifting the \nmost unstable parcel suggest limited (20%) potential. Farther north, \nan upper disturbance may (20%) kick off a light shower or two in the \nevening. Either way, am not expecting strong or severe hail/winds in \nour CWA due to the aforementioned limited instability.\n\nVery high chance for rain Thursday\n\nUpper low currently over southern Nevada will trek northeastward \nduring the middle of the week, phasing with a northern stream wave \nas it moves over the central and northern Plains on Thursday with \nthe combined upper trough advancing over WI late Thursday night. \nAssociated surface low will develop in the TX Panhandle and quickly \nmove toward the Great Lakes. Southern stream upper low looks to \nbring a good amount of moisture with it so, while low level moist \nadvection from the Gulf will be limited in time within our CWA, will \nvery likely (80-95%) get rain Thursday regardless. 13.13z NBM mean \namounts are around a half inch and max 24 hour rain amounts across \nguidance are around 3\", too low for flooding concerns. Thus, am not \nexpecting much in the way of impacts with this system.\n\nFrost and freeze possible Saturday morning\n\nBroad surface high builds in the wake of Thursday's upper trough \nwith clearing skies and light winds leading to excellent radiational \ncooling across a wide swath of the Midwest. In our forecast area \ntemperatures should tumble into the 30s with some 20s possible in \nour northeast. That said, am still concerned that we may struggle to \nfall as far as would otherwise be possible given 13.12z cycle \nguidance continuing to show a light surface pressure gradient and \nthus a light breeze being maintained through the night. While a \nfrost appears very likely (70-100%) with a good chance for a freeze \n(40-90%), the potential (20-50%) to drop below 28 north of I-94 and \nsurely end the growing season has trended to cover less area \ncompared to previous forecast updates.",
